The pane is drawn where the scene draws the window, not where the workspace says.

A workspace that slides out keeps the offset it slid out to: a whole monitor
up or down (or sideways), for as long as it is not shown. Hyprland adds that
offset to a window only in the monitor's own scene; a scene of one workspace's
windows (a snapshot, or a view that draws several workspaces at once:
stackzoom) is drawn without it. The glass read the workspace's offset itself,
whatever the scene, so in such a view every pane of a workspace that had been
left was a row away from its window: over the terminals of the row above after
a step down, over the row below after a step up, or off the screen.

- draw() hands the pass the offset Hyprland gave it for this scene
  (workspace plus floating offset of the presentation), and renderPass(),
  the pass's bounding box and its live-blur question all take the box from
  that. In the monitor's scene it is the same number as before.
- A window in real fullscreen has no decorations drawn and gets its draw()
  from the render stage, which says nothing of the scene: a workspace that
  is not shown and whose offset has the window off the monitor is taken
  for a scene of its own.
- Frost coverage is still measured where the windows are in the monitor's
  scene (own box and the others' alike).

